From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Local build <local@localhost>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 00:45:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] EXP drm: Night Light after blending, so sRGB windows keep their planes
 (KWIN_NIGHT_LIGHT_AFTER_BLENDING, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-night-light-after-blending)

Night Light gives the blending space a warm white point (applyNightLight). Every sRGB window then needs a chromatic
adaptation, a full 3x3 matrix, to reach that space. So under Night Light a window on its own plane is converted by
the plane's colour pipeline, or it is composited:
- On the Arc Pro B50, the plane pipeline (1D LUT 128, 3x4 matrix, 1D LUT 32) gave visibly wrong colours (white
  contrast, black flashes; KDE bug 525839 has the same with games on AMD and NVIDIA).
- Composited, a maximized window or a VM costs 2-4 ms more per key.

With the switch, in SDR with the built-in sRGB profile, the scene blends in the output's own colour description, and
the CRTC applies Night Light as a per-channel scale in linear light (TF, multiplier, inverse TF), as KWin 5 and
GNOME did. Every factor is at most 1, so nothing clips and the scene needs no dimming:
- sRGB windows need no conversion: their planes stay under Night Light without plane colour pipelines, and a window
  looks the same on a plane and composited.
- The blending colours stay constant across Night Light steps, so the new pipeline is presented with a repaint of its
  own, and colour-managed clients no longer get a new preferred description at each step.

White and the greys come out the same as before (checked against KWin's colour code at 5500, 4500, 3500 and 2700 K).
Saturated colours are scaled per channel instead of adapted: at 4500 K, pure green (0, 255, 0) becomes (0, 221, 0)
instead of (101, 221, 0), and (230, 153, 115) becomes (230, 133, 85) instead of (220, 132, 81).

HDR, ICC profiles, "prefer color accuracy" and a CRTC that refuses the pipeline keep the previous path. Unset = as
before. $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-night-light-after-blending holding 0 or 1 decides instead while it exists. It is read
at each colour change (a Night Light step, a brightness change, an output change), so an A/B in a running session
writes the file, then turns Night Light off and on.
---
diff --git a/src/backends/drm/drm_output.cpp b/src/backends/drm/drm_output.cpp
index 207654f..60df906 100644
--- a/src/backends/drm/drm_output.cpp
+++ b/src/backends/drm/drm_output.cpp
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include "utils/kernel.h"
 // Qt
 #include <QCryptographicHash>
+#include <QFile>
 #include <QMatrix4x4>
 // c++
 #include <cerrno>
@@ -741,6 +742,35 @@ void DrmOutput::setChannelFactors(const QVector3D &rgb)
     setState(next);
 }
 
+/**
+ * KWIN_NIGHT_LIGHT_AFTER_BLENDING (EXP, 2026-11-01; unset or 0 = as before): in SDR with the built-in sRGB profile,
+ * Night Light is applied only after blending, as a per-channel scale in linear light in the CRTC's colour pipeline
+ * (the classic Night Light of KWin 5 and GNOME), and the scene blends in the output's own colour description. As
+ * before, Night Light gives the blending space a warm white point, so every sRGB window needs a chromatic adaptation
+ * (a full 3x3 matrix) to reach it: a window can then only keep its own plane through the plane's colour pipeline (on
+ * the Arc Pro B50, a 1D LUT of 128 entries, a 3x4 matrix and a 1D LUT of 32 entries, with visibly wrong colours) or is
+ * composited (2-4 ms more per key for a maximized window or a VM). With the switch, sRGB windows need no conversion,
+ * so they keep their planes under Night Light, and a window looks the same on a plane and composited. White and the
+ * greys come out the same as before; saturated colours are scaled per channel instead of adapted (at 4500 K, pure green
+ * (0, 255, 0) becomes (0, 221, 0) instead of (101, 221, 0)). HDR, ICC profiles and "prefer color accuracy" keep the
+ * previous path, and so does a CRTC that can't take the pipeline. $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-night-light-after-blending
+ * holding 0 or 1 decides instead while it exists. It is read at each colour change (a Night Light step, a brightness
+ * change, an output change), so an A/B in a running session writes the file, then turns Night Light off and on
+ */
+static bool nightLightAfterBlending()
+{
+    bool value = qEnvironmentVariableIntValue("KWIN_NIGHT_LIGHT_AFTER_BLENDING") != 0;
+    QFile file(qEnvironmentVariable("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR") + QStringLiteral("/kwin-night-light-after-blending"));
+    if (file.open(QIODevice::ReadOnly)) {
+        bool ok = false;
+        const int fileValue = file.readAll().trimmed().toInt(&ok);
+        if (ok) {
+            value = fileValue != 0;
+        }
+    }
+    return value;
+}
+
 void DrmOutput::tryKmsColorOffloading(State &next)
 {
     if (!m_pipeline->activePending() || m_pipeline->layers().empty() || m_lease) {
@@ -786,6 +816,33 @@ void DrmOutput::tryKmsColorOffloading(State &next)
             || !colorPipeline.isIdentity();
         return;
     }
+    if (!usesICC && !hdr && profileSource == ColorProfileSource::sRGB
+        && next.originalColorDescription->transferFunction().type == blendingSpace
+        && m_sRgbChannelFactors != QVector3D(1, 1, 1) && nightLightAfterBlending()) {
+        // EXP (KWIN_NIGHT_LIGHT_AFTER_BLENDING): blend in the output's own colour description and let the CRTC scale
+        // each channel in linear light. The factors are at most 1, so nothing clips and no dimming is needed
+        const auto &neutral = next.originalColorDescription;
+        ColorPipeline tint(ValueRange{0, 1}, ColorspaceType::NonLinearRGB);
+        tint.addTransferFunction(neutral->transferFunction(), ColorspaceType::LinearRGB);
+        tint.addMultiplier(adaptChannelFactors(neutral, m_sRgbChannelFactors));
+        tint.addInverseTransferFunction(neutral->transferFunction(), ColorspaceType::NonLinearRGB);
+        m_pipeline->setCrtcColorPipeline(tint);
+        if (DrmPipeline::commitPipelines({m_pipeline}, m_gpu, DrmPipeline::CommitMode::Test)) {
+            m_pipeline->applyPendingChanges();
+            if (!(tint == m_baseCrtcColorPipeline)) {
+                // the blending colours stay the output's own across Night Light steps, so maybeScheduleRepaints
+                // sees no change: present the new pipeline anyway
+                for (const auto &layer : m_pipeline->layers()) {
+                    layer->addDeviceRepaint(Region::infinite());
+                }
+            }
+            next.blendingColor = neutral;
+            next.layerBlendingColor = neutral;
+            m_needsShadowBuffer = false;
+            return;
+        }
+        // the CRTC can't take it: as before
+    }
     if (usesICC) {
         colorPipeline.addTransferFunction(encoding->transferFunction(), ColorspaceType::LinearRGB);
